        <div class="card">
            <h2>Key Aotearoa Youth Statistics</h2>
            <p>Comprehensive national surveys provide clear, statistically rigorous data regarding how Kiwi teenagers interact with digital media and technology daily:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>90% Usage:</strong> 90% of New Zealand teenagers actively use social media platforms, spending an average of 2.5 hours online every day.</li>
                <li><strong>Problematic Use:</strong> Approximately 22% of NZ teens meet clinical or research criteria for problematic, addictive-like social media use.</li>
                <li><strong>Daily Disruption:</strong> Almost half (49%) report that social media frequently disrupts essential daily activities, including sleep schedules, household chores, and homework.</li>
                <li><strong>Digital Harassment:</strong> 7 in 10 teens in New Zealand have experienced at least one type of unwanted digital communication or harassment over the past year.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>Impacts on Mental Health and Sleep</h2>
            <p>The <strong>Youth19 Survey</strong>—the largest and most comprehensive survey of youth health in Aotearoa—highlights direct correlations between excessive late-night screen time and heightened anxiety, depression symptoms, and severe sleep deprivation among adolescents. When devices remain in bedrooms overnight, blue light exposure and notification alerts interrupt natural circadian rhythms, compounding mental fatigue.</p>

